From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hero Siege Offline Toolkit <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] runner interface: refuse to publish an interface the walk
 did not find

Why: GmpRunnerInterfaceHook replays the game's interface-construction
chain into a private 4-page copy of the stack and then copies the runner
interface out of it. Upstream does neither step with a check: each store
goes to base + whatever displacement the instruction carries (its own
comment says "let's pray the stack isn't this big"), and the final copy
runs from offset 0 even when the walk never reached the rsp-relative lea
that says where the interface is. On a game build whose chain the walk
misreads, that is a heap write outside the buffer followed by a runner
interface made of whatever bytes were there - and stage 2 then calls
through it. Every store and the final copy are now checked against the
buffer, and the interface is published only if the walk ended on the
closing lea, the interface lies inside the buffer and no store of the
chain was refused.

Evidence: v4.0.1 Generic-RunnerInterfaceNew.cpp: the stores at the
"case ZYDIS_REGISTER_RSP / RBP" switch and the unconditional memcpy after
the loop. Found by review of patch 0002 of this series, which introduced
the is_chain_end_found flag but only used it to word a log line. The
frame measured on Hero Siege (rsp = rbp - 0x100, stores from rsp+0x60 to
rbp+0x248, 100 stores) fits the buffer with room to spare, so on that
build this patch changes nothing but the summary line. That a zeroed
interface ends stage 2 in a status rather than a fault is read from
source: both functions-array finders return AURIE_MODULE_INTERNAL_ERROR
on a null Code_Function_Find (YYC-Functions.cpp, VM-Functions.cpp) - it
has NOT been observed, because no launch has produced a refusal.
YYToolkit/hs-tests/ri_dump_test.cpp: 16 new checks of the bounds
predicate, including the measured offsets as the positive control and
INT64_MIN / INT64_MAX and a one-byte overhang as negative controls.

Fails-safe: A refusal leaves m_RunnerInterface zeroed, writes one line
naming which of the three conditions failed and how many stores were
refused, and still signals the waiting stage 2, which then fails with a
status instead of calling through garbage. The hook runs once per launch,
so this is one line. The "RI init summary:" line gains the refused-store
count, so a run that published the interface shows "0 refused".

Log-markers: "REFUSED to publish the runner interface"

Upstream-status: not submitted. Upstream v4.0.1 has neither check.

diff --git a/YYToolkit/hs-tests/ri_dump_test.cpp b/YYToolkit/hs-tests/ri_dump_test.cpp
index 17fd320..87f7ca0 100644
--- a/YYToolkit/hs-tests/ri_dump_test.cpp
+++ b/YYToolkit/hs-tests/ri_dump_test.cpp
@@ -107,10 +107,42 @@ static void TestRowCount()
 	Check(CountNonZeroRows(edges.data(), 0, row_size) == 0, "rows: no bytes, no rows");
 }
 
+static void TestInsideBuffer()
+{
+	// The hook's private stack is 4 pages, its bases sit 2 pages in, a store is 8 bytes
+	constexpr size_t buffer_size = 4 * 0x1000;
+	constexpr int64_t base = 2 * 0x1000;
+	constexpr size_t store = sizeof(uint64_t);
+
+	// The frame measured on Hero Siege: rsp = rbp - 0x100, stores from rsp+0x60 up to rbp+0x248
+	Check(IsInsideBuffer(base - 0x100 + 0x60, store, buffer_size), "bounds: the measured first store is inside");
+	Check(IsInsideBuffer(base + 0x248, store, buffer_size), "bounds: the measured last store is inside");
+
+	Check(IsInsideBuffer(0, store, buffer_size), "bounds: the first 8 bytes are inside");
+	Check(IsInsideBuffer(buffer_size - store, store, buffer_size), "bounds: the last 8 bytes are inside");
+	Check(!IsInsideBuffer(buffer_size - store + 1, store, buffer_size), "bounds: one byte past the end is refused");
+	Check(!IsInsideBuffer(buffer_size, store, buffer_size), "bounds: a store at the end is refused");
+	Check(!IsInsideBuffer(-1, store, buffer_size), "bounds: one byte before the start is refused");
+	Check(!IsInsideBuffer(base - 3 * 0x1000, store, buffer_size), "bounds: a displacement of -3 pages is refused");
+	Check(!IsInsideBuffer(base + 2 * 0x1000, store, buffer_size), "bounds: a displacement of +2 pages is refused");
+
+	// Extremes must not wrap into an accepted offset
+	Check(!IsInsideBuffer(INT64_MIN, store, buffer_size), "bounds: INT64_MIN is refused");
+	Check(!IsInsideBuffer(INT64_MAX, store, buffer_size), "bounds: INT64_MAX is refused");
+
+	// The copy of the whole interface is one large access
+	Check(IsInsideBuffer(base - 0x100 + 0x60, 0x800, buffer_size), "bounds: a large copy from the measured offset is inside");
+	Check(!IsInsideBuffer(buffer_size - 0x7FF, 0x800, buffer_size), "bounds: a large copy that overhangs by one byte is refused");
+	Check(!IsInsideBuffer(0, buffer_size + 1, buffer_size), "bounds: an access larger than the buffer is refused");
+	Check(IsInsideBuffer(0, buffer_size, buffer_size), "bounds: an access of exactly the buffer is inside");
+	Check(!IsInsideBuffer(0, 0, buffer_size), "bounds: an empty access is refused");
+}
+
 int main()
 {
 	TestDumpGate();
 	TestRowCount();
+	TestInsideBuffer();
 
 	std::printf("%d checks, %d failed\n", g_ChecksRun, g_ChecksFailed);
 	return g_ChecksFailed ? 1 : 0;
